markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Data Privacy in Practice

#### Now that you’ve explored key considerations for data privacy when using AI tools, this activity focuses on applying that understanding to real classroom situations.

#### In each scenario, a teacher or student is using an AI tool. Your job is to identify what data privacy concerns are present and what steps could be taken to use the tool more responsibly.

#### 👉 Review each scenario and try to identify what data privacy concerns exist before clicking to reveal the explanation.

>#### **💬 Quick Reflection:** What data privacy concerns do you have in each of these situations?

--- 

<div style="max-width:900px; margin:20px auto; font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; color:#292F36;">

<!-- Scenario 1 -->
<details style="margin-bottom:12px; border:1px solid #D9DDE3; border-radius:10px; overflow:hidden;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer; padding:16px 18px; background-color:#F7F8FA; list-style:none;">
<div style="font-size:18px; font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px;">Scenario 1</div>
<div style="font-size:16px; color:#555; margin-bottom:10px;">
A student is using an AI tool to help draft a resume for a summer job. They enter their full name, contact information, school, and details about past experiences into the tool to generate a polished version.
</div>
</summary>
<div style="padding:18px; font-size:18px; line-height:1.5;">
<p style="font-size:16px;">
This is a common real-world use, but it involves sharing personally identifiable information in a tool that may store or reuse inputs. Before encouraging this use, check whether the tool explicitly states how it handles personal data and whether it’s appropriate for minors. A safer approach is to have students use placeholders (e.g., “First Name,” “School Name”) and only add real details after finalizing the document.
</p>
</div>
</details>

<!-- Scenario 2 -->
<details style="margin-bottom:12px; border:1px solid #D9DDE3; border-radius:10px; overflow:hidden;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer; padding:16px 18px; background-color:#F7F8FA; list-style:none;">
<div style="font-size:18px; font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px;">Scenario 2</div>
<div style="font-size:16px; color:#555; margin-bottom:10px;">
A special education teacher is drafting an Individualized Education Program (IEP) and uses an AI tool to help write sections more efficiently. They copy and paste detailed student information, including learning needs, behavioral notes, accommodations, and identifying information into the tool.
</div>
</summary>
<div style="padding:18px; font-size:18px; line-height:1.5;">
<p style="font-size:16px;">
This is high-risk because IEPs contain protected student information under FERPA. Even if the tool is convenient, it should not be used unless it is district-approved and clearly states how it complies with student data privacy requirements. For this reason, many districts restrict or prohibit the use of AI tools for drafting or developing IEPs.
</p>
</div>
</details>

<!-- Scenario 3 -->
<details style="margin-bottom:12px; border:1px solid #D9DDE3; border-radius:10px; overflow:hidden;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer; padding:16px 18px; background-color:#F7F8FA; list-style:none;">
<div style="font-size:18px; font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px;">Scenario 3</div>
<div style="font-size:16px; color:#555; margin-bottom:10px;">
Students are using an AI image generator to create visuals for a project. One student uploads a photo of themselves to create different versions, while another uploads a photo of a classmate without their permission. The tool generates realistic images, which are then shared among peers.
</div>
</summary>
<div style="padding:18px; font-size:18px; line-height:1.5;">
<p style="font-size:16px;">
This scenario raises important issues around both privacy and consent. Students should never upload a photo of another person without their explicit permission, and many do not realize that once an image is uploaded, it may be stored or reused by the tool. Teachers should set clear expectations about asking permission before sharing images. In many cases, it may be advisable to avoid using identifiable photos altogether unless the tool’s privacy practices are well understood.
</p>
</div>
</details>

<!-- Scenario 4 -->
<details style="margin-bottom:12px; border:1px solid #D9DDE3; border-radius:10px; overflow:hidden;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer; padding:16px 18px; background-color:#F7F8FA; list-style:none;">
<div style="font-size:18px; font-weight:600; margin-bottom:6px;">Scenario 4</div>
<div style="font-size:16px; color:#555; margin-bottom:10px;">
A teacher is planning a lesson and decides to use an AI tool to help group students based on recent performance. They copy and paste an entire spreadsheet from their LMS into the tool, including student names, ID numbers, scores, and notes about strengths and challenges.
</div>
</summary>
<div style="padding:18px; font-size:18px; line-height:1.5;">
<p style="font-size:16px;">
This is a direct violation of data minimization—far more information is being shared than necessary. Before using an AI tool for this purpose, remove all identifiers (names, IDs) and reduce inputs to patterns (e.g., “Group students with similar performance trends”). Even then, confirm whether the tool is approved and whether its privacy policy clearly states that data is not stored or reused.
</p>
</div>
</details>

<br>
</div>
MARKDOWN
